                Tightness of contact Godbillon–Vey forms:
         a classical argument for Calegari’s conditional question
                                               Alec Kriebel
                                         Independent researcher
                                       ORCID: 0009-0001-9320-500X

                                             5 October 2026


                                                  Abstract
         If a global deﬁning form α of a cooriented taut C 2 foliation on a closed three-manifold satisﬁes
     dα = α ∧ ω with a smooth contact form ω, then ker ω is tight. The proof combines a special case
     of Dathe–Khoule’s 2012 general aﬃne-deformation criterion with the Eliashberg–Thurston tight-
     ness neighborhood theorem and smooth Gray stability. A ﬁnite-interval smoothing argument
     allows α to be only C 1 . This is a classical corollary, not a claim of a new contact construction
     or a ﬁrst historical solution. Priority remains unresolved.


1    The conditional question and the precise claim
The target is Question 13.2 on printed p. 29 of Calegari’s actual 2002 author preprint, dated 8
September, Version 0.78 [1]. Its antecedents are inherited from Question 13.1: F is a minimal taut
C 2 foliation of an atoroidal three-manifold M , and gv(F)[M ] ̸= 0. One additionally supposes that
a choice of global deﬁning form α makes the associated one-form ω contact, with the convention
                                      T F = ker α,        dα = α ∧ ω.                                        (1)
The requested conclusion is that the contact structure ker ω is tight. We use the closed, oriented
interpretation supplied by a nonzero evaluation on the ordinary fundamental class. This is an
interpretation of that evaluation, not a quoted source-wide convention; no boundary or noncompact
version is asserted.
Question 13.1 separately asks whether the deﬁning form can be chosen so that ω ∧ dω has one weak
sign everywhere. Neither that existence question nor existence of a contact ω is established here.
The ﬁnal AMS chapter published in 2003 has DOI 10.1090/pspum/071/2024640; its full text has
not been read, and equality with the 2002 wording is not presumed.
Theorem 1. Let M be a closed oriented smooth three-manifold, and let F be a cooriented taut
C 2 foliation without spherical leaves. Suppose α is a global nowhere-zero C 1 one-form deﬁning
F, and ω is a smooth contact one-form satisfying (1). Then ker ω is tight in the usual smooth
contact-topological sense.

Minimality in the source excludes spherical leaves. Atoroidality, minimality, and the numerical
Godbillon–Vey value play no further role in this implication. The global deﬁning form supplies the
coorientation. The conclusion therefore applies to the speciﬁed 2002 conditional question under
the smooth contact-form interpretation.

                                                      1
2    Proof by a ﬁnite contact path
We use two classical inputs. First, every smooth contact structure suﬃciently C 0 close to the
tangent planes of a taut C 2 foliation without spherical leaves on a closed three-manifold is tight.
This Eliashberg–Thurston neighborhood statement is explicitly recorded in Vogel [4, p. 42] and
Dathe–Rukimbira [6, Proposition 3.3, p. 5]; the latter refers to p. 50 of Confoliations [3]. We rely
on those actual primary-paper statements, rather than claiming a full reading of the book. For
negative contact structures apply the same result after reversing the orientation of the relevant
component. Second, smooth Gray stability identiﬁes every member of a smooth family of smooth
contact structures on a closed manifold by an isotopy [5, Theorem 2.9, p. 2451]. Tightness is
consequently constant on such a path.

Proof. Diﬀerentiating (1) gives

                               0 = d2 α = dα ∧ ω − α ∧ dω = −α ∧ dω.                                 (2)

This calculation is valid when α is only C 1 : d2 α = 0 distributionally, α ∧ ω is C 1 , and its product
rule yields the continuous form α ∧ dω. A continuous form that vanishes distributionally vanishes
pointwise. For each constant real s, deﬁne βs = ω + sα. Then

                        βs ∧ dβs = ω ∧ dω + s(ω ∧ dα + α ∧ dω) + s2 α ∧ dα
                                  = ω ∧ dω.                                                          (3)

Thus every βs is contact, with the same sign. Moreover,

                 ker βs = ker(α + s−1 ω) −→ ker α       uniformly in C 0   (s −→ +∞).                (4)

Compactness and the positive minimum of |α| justify this uniform plane-ﬁeld convergence. Choose
a ﬁnite S > 0 for which ker βS lies in the tightness neighborhood just described.
If α is smooth, (3) gives a smooth contact path on [0, S]. Its endpoint is tight, so smooth Gray
stability proves the claim at s = 0.
For C 1 α, keep this same ﬁnite S and keep ω unchanged. Approximate α in C 1 by a smooth
one-form a, using smooth charts and a partition of unity, and consider

                                     λs = ω + sa,        0 ≤ s ≤ S.                                  (5)

Here is a uniform contact bound. Choose a metric and, component by component, an auxiliary
orientation making ω ∧ dω = c(x)dV with c ≥ c0 > 0. On M × [0, S], bound both |βs | and |dβs | by
B. If the one-form and exterior-derivative errors of (5) are at most δ, their contact-volume error is
at most 2Bδ + δ 2 . Choose δ with this bound below c0 /2; the approximation can make the errors
at most δ uniformly because S is ﬁxed. Every λs is then contact. Also ker λS remains inside the
C 0 tightness neighborhood by taking the approximation suﬃciently small. Smooth Gray stability
along (5) transports its tightness to ker λ0 = ker ω.

The approximation a need not deﬁne a foliation or satisfy (1). Only closeness on a ﬁxed ﬁnite
interval is used. Gray stability is never applied to a C 1 path, to s = ∞, or to a foliated endpoint.
Reversing an auxiliary orientation does not change tightness of the underlying contact planes.



                                                    2
3    Classical attribution and the limits of the note
Dathe–Khoule’s general aﬃne criterion [2, Deﬁnition 2.1 and Theorem 2.2, pp. 102–103] already
covers the contact mechanism. In dimension three, for an integrable deﬁning form α and a positive
contact form ω, its criterion is
                                   Q = α ∧ dω + ω ∧ dα ≥ 0.                                   (6)
Their deﬁnition permits C(t) = 1, B(t) = t, giving α + tω. In the present case each term of Q is
zero, and their expansion specializes to

                            (α + tω) ∧ d(α + tω) = t2 ω ∧ dω      (t > 0).

After positive scaling, these are precisely the planes of ω + sα with s = 1/t. The criterion assumes
integrability, not closedness of α, and allows zero mixed term; no strictly positive ﬁrst variation is
needed. Negative sign is handled by orientation reversal.
The original 2012 statement was read in an author-uploaded article transcription; its PDF pixels
were not obtained. It does not itself print a C 1 threshold. The later coauthor preprint [7, §6,
Theorem 6.2, pp. 32–33] explicitly attributes the C 1 criterion to the 2012 work. Its normalized-
equality display after equation (62) omits the general nonnegative mixed term and should be an
inequality. Equality is correct for Q = 0, the only case used here; (3) independently veriﬁes it. No
unrelated main result of that 2025 preprint is endorsed.
Theorem 1 is our stated corollary of prior aﬃne, Eliashberg–Thurston, and Gray results. The
near-foliation/isotopy tightness argument already occurs with a closed deﬁning form in Dathe–
Rukimbira [6, Proposition 3.4]; that narrower statement alone does not cover nonclosed α. No literal
statement of the exact tightness answer to Calegari’s Question 13.2 was located in the complete
transcribed 2012 article or the relevant 2025 section. This bounded absence is not ﬁrstness evidence.
The ﬁnal 2003 chapter, the full Confoliations book, the ﬁnal 2011 Dathe–Rukimbira article, and
other identiﬁed literature remain unread. Historical priority, novelty of any explicit application or
regularity reﬁnement, and present-day open status are unresolved.
The portable support includes the longer current candidate, which separately treats C 1 ω using an
explicitly deﬁned embedded C 2 -disk criterion and tightness of suﬃciently C 1 -close smooth contact
forms. Those qualiﬁed extras are not claims of this short theorem, and are not identiﬁed with
every low-regularity deﬁnition of tightness. Exact symbolic and rational diagnostics check wedge
identities, sign conventions, smoothing corrections, and ﬁnite-interval margins. They neither prove
the imported global contact-topology theorems nor certify priority.
AI tools were used extensively in analysis, drafting, source comparison, and veriﬁcation. No con-
ventional human peer review or refereeing has occurred. Independent AI audits are not human
peer review.




